In article <364867DB.FE908C47@mbox4.singnet.com.sg>, thomas meier <backball@mbox4.singnet.com.sg> writes:
>we are trying to remove data from openvms systems using the $ init/erase
>command. the department of defense (DoD) has a standard that says if a
>data carrier is overwritten at least three times with a (random) pattern
>the possibilities of retrieving the original information are very very
>low.

But not zero, see below.

>
>does anyone of you know what the $ init/erase command exactly does or
>whether there's another DCL command fulfilling the DoD standard?

I don't know what init/erase does exactly, but a tiny C or fortran
program could do what you want if it is run on a freshly init'd disk.
It would just write until the disk was full, then writes it twice more.
Then you reinit.  Or with priv's you could skip the init and just
use write physical to obliterate the disk.


That said, I'm not sure that any OS level overwriting of disks satisfies
the DoD standards, as disks can transparently remap failed blocks. Monica's
phone number might still be readable on a "bad block", and there's nothing
you can do from the OS level to erase it.  Yet, the data could be read by
someone with enough determination and unlimited physical access to the
device.  The disk's manufacturer may have a specialized diagnostic program
which will let you access these swapped blocks though.  (Ever seen such a 
thing Glenn?)

Failing that, there's always the crusher...

