thomas meier wrote:
> 
> we are trying to remove data from openvms systems using the $ init/erase
> command. the department of defense (DoD) has a standard that says if a
> data carrier is overwritten at least three times with a (random) pattern
> the possibilities of retrieving the original information are very very
> low.
> 
> does anyone of you know what the $ init/erase command exactly does or
> whether there's another DCL command fulfilling the DoD standard?

By default, INIT/ERASE overwrites the disk once with zeroes. You can
modify this behavior by plugging into the $ERAPAT system service, which
specifies to the file system and other components what pattern sequence
to use for erases. As starting point, see section 8.9.5.1, "Erasing
Techniques", in the OpenVMS Guide to System Security. There is a sample
module, SYS$EXAMPLES:DOD_ERAPAT.MAR, that provides the most commonly
accepted 3-pass erase pattern.



