In article <364867DB.FE908C47@mbox4.singnet.com.sg>, thomas meier <backball@mbox4.singnet.com.sg> writes:
:we are trying to remove data from openvms systems using the $ init/erase
:command. the department of defense (DoD) has a standard that says if a
:data carrier is overwritten at least three times with a (random) pattern
:the possibilities of retrieving the original information are very very
:low.
:
:does anyone of you know what the $ init/erase command exactly does or
:whether there's another DCL command fulfilling the DoD standard?

  The loadable sys$erapat support module (look in the sys$examples: 
  area on OpenVMS VAX) is the erase pattern tool, and it can be used 
  (on VAX) with commands such as INITIALIZE/ERASE.  (Off the top, I
  do not know if this support was folded into OpenVMS Alpha -- I know 
  that the source code example module was not.)

  Erasing a disk is rather more complex than simply overwriting the
  sectors, and you may or may not be able to use overwriting to meet 
  the requirements for a US DoD data security erase.  Things like
  revectored or bad sectors come into play, as do track-skewed reads
  and various more sophisticated devices for pulling data off media.

  Without knowing the specific sort of storage hardware involved and
  without knowing the site security officer's specific requirements
  for security, answering the question is effectively impossible.

  INITIALIZE/ERASE will satisfy some site security officers, and only
  the pattern erasure, bulk degaussing, physical media destruction, and 
  then slagging of the resulting fragments of the storage media into a
  puddle of molten goo -- I am not making this up -- will satisfy other
  officers.  (I've heard claim of one site that posted armed guards around 
  the resulting slag -- having been on the wrong end of the M16s at one 
  site (innocently), this is not a claim I would particularly dispute.)

  This question -- secure data erasure -- comes up with some regularity, 
  and there is (still) no good (general) answer.  Please contact your 
  local hardware support organization or the customer support center for 
  further assistance for your particular situation.

