As I think I mentioned here some months ago, there is some relevant basic
physics:  all permanently magnetizable materials have a critical 
temperature, known as the Curie Temperature, below which there is permanent
magnetization, and above which there is not.  Any portion of a magnetic 
medium that has been above its Curie Temperature is in a state that carries 
*NO* information about its previous state. 

The magnetic medium on a disk or tape may not be uniform in composition,
and is not likely to be uniform in temperature (because of variations in
insulation and heat capacity of the surrounding materials).  Those
non-uniformities are likely to be quite modest.  Consequently, if the
Curie Temperature of the medium is known, baking for an hour at fifteen
degrees higher oven temperature should be utterly effective at erasing
all data, including re-vectored blocks.  If the substrate of the medium
melts at a lower temperature, that also would destroy the information, 
because the magnetization carries information only by its physical 
location, which would be disrupted when you stirred the soup. 

As Hoff says, though, the issue is whether the site security officer has
faith in basic physics, or not.

